1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,522
Your app works perfectly on your laptop.

2
00:00:02,801 --> 00:00:03,996
You put it on a server,

3
00:00:04,031 --> 00:00:05,134
and it crashes.

4
00:00:05,413 --> 00:00:07,433
The server has a different Node version.

5
00:00:07,758 --> 00:00:09,163
A library is missing.

6
00:00:09,488 --> 00:00:10,765
A setting is different.

7
00:00:11,148 --> 00:00:12,739
Docker's promise is simple:

8
00:00:13,064 --> 00:00:14,062
pack the app together

9
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,536
with everything it needs,

10
00:00:15,711 --> 00:00:17,893
so it runs the same everywhere.

11
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:20,914
Docker calls that package a container.

12
00:00:21,123 --> 00:00:22,458
And here's the strange part.

13
00:00:22,737 --> 00:00:23,933
A container acts like

14
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:25,384
its own little computer,

15
00:00:25,465 --> 00:00:27,752
with its own files and its own network.

16
00:00:27,996 --> 00:00:29,703
Yet it starts in about a second,

17
00:00:29,796 --> 00:00:32,037
and your laptop can run dozens of them.

18
00:00:32,478 --> 00:00:34,069
How can something act like a whole

19
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:36,205
computer and cost almost nothing?

20
00:00:36,646 --> 00:00:37,888
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:38,004 --> 00:00:39,781
you'll know exactly how.

22
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:41,357
Let's pretend we're

23
00:00:41,381 --> 00:00:42,948
building Docker ourselves.

24
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:45,340
To run safely next to other apps,

25
00:00:45,456 --> 00:00:47,209
our app needs four things.

26
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:49,508
One: its own files,

27
00:00:49,542 --> 00:00:51,597
in the exact versions it was built with.

28
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:55,289
Two: it can't see or bump into other apps.

29
00:00:55,672 --> 00:00:58,343
Three: it can't hog the whole machine.

30
00:00:58,668 --> 00:01:00,398
And four: it has to be

31
00:01:00,456 --> 00:01:02,592
cheap and quick to start.

32
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,800
The obvious answer is a virtual machine.

33
00:01:06,125 --> 00:01:07,646
That's a pretend computer,

34
00:01:07,797 --> 00:01:08,946
made out of software,

35
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:10,571
running inside your real one.

36
00:01:10,896 --> 00:01:13,218
Give every app its own pretend computer,

37
00:01:13,277 --> 00:01:15,436
with a full operating system inside,

38
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:16,608
and the apps can't

39
00:01:16,655 --> 00:01:18,257
get in each other's way.

40
00:01:19,017 --> 00:01:19,934
One word matters for

41
00:01:19,981 --> 00:01:21,084
the rest of this video,

42
00:01:21,153 --> 00:01:22,500
so let's zoom in.

43
00:01:22,825 --> 00:01:24,485
At the center of every operating

44
00:01:24,543 --> 00:01:26,831
system is a program called the kernel.

45
00:01:27,074 --> 00:01:29,013
Think of it as the computer's manager.

46
00:01:29,257 --> 00:01:30,883
It starts every program,

47
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:32,717
hands out memory, and decides

48
00:01:32,752 --> 00:01:34,331
who can touch which files.

49
00:01:34,609 --> 00:01:35,492
And a program that's

50
00:01:35,538 --> 00:01:36,931
running has a name too:

51
00:01:37,256 --> 00:01:38,615
a process.

52
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:40,934
So with virtual machines,

53
00:01:41,108 --> 00:01:43,001
every app gets its own operating

54
00:01:43,059 --> 00:01:44,870
system and its own kernel.

55
00:01:45,253 --> 00:01:47,389
That checks the first three boxes.

56
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:49,154
But three apps means three

57
00:01:49,235 --> 00:01:50,710
full operating systems,

58
00:01:50,884 --> 00:01:52,590
each one booting from scratch,

59
00:01:52,741 --> 00:01:54,750
each one using gigabytes of memory.

60
00:01:55,191 --> 00:01:58,407
Box four, cheap and quick, fails.

61
00:01:59,167 --> 00:02:00,816
So let's ask a better question.

62
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:03,532
Does our app really need its own computer?

63
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:04,926
Or does it only need

64
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,214
to believe it has one?

65
00:02:06,539 --> 00:02:08,943
Remember: the kernel is the manager.

66
00:02:09,152 --> 00:02:10,231
It already decides

67
00:02:10,255 --> 00:02:11,914
what every process gets.

68
00:02:12,123 --> 00:02:13,551
So what if the kernel simply

69
00:02:13,609 --> 00:02:15,838
showed our app a smaller world?

70
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,829
Here's what a normal process sees today.

71
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:20,989
Every other program on the machine,

72
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:22,161
each one with an ID

73
00:02:22,312 --> 00:02:23,578
number the kernel gave it.

74
00:02:23,903 --> 00:02:25,818
That number is called a PID,

75
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:27,420
short for process ID.

76
00:02:27,746 --> 00:02:29,952
It also sees every file on the disk,

77
00:02:29,975 --> 00:02:31,775
and every network connection.

78
00:02:32,158 --> 00:02:33,458
Nothing is hidden.

79
00:02:34,217 --> 00:02:36,493
Linux has a feature for exactly this,

80
00:02:36,585 --> 00:02:38,211
called namespaces.

81
00:02:38,594 --> 00:02:40,684
Think of a namespace as blinders.

82
00:02:41,009 --> 00:02:42,983
It changes what a process can see,

83
00:02:43,064 --> 00:02:44,039
and nothing else.

84
00:02:44,422 --> 00:02:45,839
Put blinders on our app,

85
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:47,684
and the other programs vanish.

86
00:02:47,928 --> 00:02:50,320
It even believes it's process number one,

87
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:52,862
the very first program on the machine.

88
00:02:53,245 --> 00:02:54,081
That's a lie.

89
00:02:54,465 --> 00:02:55,811
From the outside, it's

90
00:02:55,858 --> 00:02:58,691
still process 48213.

91
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,389
The same blinders work for files.

92
00:03:01,830 --> 00:03:03,490
Point the app at one ordinary

93
00:03:03,572 --> 00:03:04,674
folder on the server,

94
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:07,066
and that folder becomes its whole world.

95
00:03:07,449 --> 00:03:08,262
When the app looks at

96
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:09,783
the top of its file system,

97
00:03:09,957 --> 00:03:12,337
all it finds is what's inside that folder.

98
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:15,007
Everything else on the disk is invisible.

99
00:03:15,767 --> 00:03:17,114
And the same for the network.

100
00:03:17,358 --> 00:03:19,563
The app gets its own network address.

101
00:03:19,842 --> 00:03:22,257
So two apps can both use port 80,

102
00:03:22,431 --> 00:03:24,115
the standard port for websites,

103
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:25,426
and they never collide.

104
00:03:25,868 --> 00:03:27,726
Each one thinks it's the only one.

105
00:03:28,109 --> 00:03:30,315
That's box two: our app can't

106
00:03:30,384 --> 00:03:32,532
see or bump into anything else.

107
00:03:33,283 --> 00:03:35,199
But blinders only hide things.

108
00:03:35,477 --> 00:03:37,230
A hidden app can still eat all

109
00:03:37,288 --> 00:03:39,239
the memory and slow everyone down.

110
00:03:39,622 --> 00:03:41,340
So Linux has a second feature,

111
00:03:41,433 --> 00:03:43,953
called control groups, or cgroups.

112
00:03:44,231 --> 00:03:45,903
A cgroup is a budget.

113
00:03:46,147 --> 00:03:47,970
Half of one CPU core.

114
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:50,384
512 megabytes of memory.

115
00:03:50,709 --> 00:03:52,706
If the app goes over its memory budget,

116
00:03:52,788 --> 00:03:54,204
the kernel shuts it down.

117
00:03:54,587 --> 00:03:56,178
That's box three.

118
00:03:56,933 --> 00:03:58,616
Now look at what we've built.

119
00:03:59,000 --> 00:03:59,870
One machine.

120
00:04:00,149 --> 00:04:01,020
One kernel.

121
00:04:01,345 --> 00:04:03,446
And a few ordinary processes,

122
00:04:03,574 --> 00:04:05,362
each with blinders and a budget.

123
00:04:05,687 --> 00:04:07,127
There's no second operating

124
00:04:07,185 --> 00:04:08,160
system to boot.

125
00:04:08,543 --> 00:04:09,587
Starting a container

126
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,457
is just starting a process,

127
00:04:11,608 --> 00:04:13,523
and that's why it takes about a second.

128
00:04:13,964 --> 00:04:14,928
Box four.

129
00:04:15,253 --> 00:04:17,053
Three boxes done, and they

130
00:04:17,122 --> 00:04:18,899
all came from the same trick:

131
00:04:19,224 --> 00:04:20,211
the kernel showing a

132
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:22,521
process a smaller world.

133
00:04:23,283 --> 00:04:24,223
One catch.

134
00:04:24,607 --> 00:04:25,965
These blinders and budgets

135
00:04:26,023 --> 00:04:27,706
are Linux kernel features.

136
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:29,506
So on a Mac or Windows,

137
00:04:29,634 --> 00:04:32,258
Docker Desktop quietly runs one small

138
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:34,394
Linux virtual machine in the background.

139
00:04:34,603 --> 00:04:37,540
Just one, shared by all your containers.

140
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:40,889
That leaves box one: the files.

141
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:43,037
Remember the crash from the beginning?

142
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:45,370
The server had the wrong files.

143
00:04:45,812 --> 00:04:46,984
So the fix is to ship

144
00:04:47,042 --> 00:04:48,285
the files with the app.

145
00:04:48,668 --> 00:04:50,537
That bundle is called an image.

146
00:04:50,862 --> 00:04:53,126
It holds a small set of Linux tools,

147
00:04:53,219 --> 00:04:54,380
the right Node version,

148
00:04:54,530 --> 00:04:56,272
your libraries, and your code.

149
00:04:56,655 --> 00:04:57,944
When the container starts,

150
00:04:58,037 --> 00:04:59,360
the image's files fill

151
00:04:59,441 --> 00:05:00,544
that private folder.

152
00:05:00,986 --> 00:05:02,263
Box one.

153
00:05:03,017 --> 00:05:05,594
Now notice what an image does not contain:

154
00:05:05,919 --> 00:05:06,697
a kernel.

155
00:05:07,139 --> 00:05:08,903
The image brings the files.

156
00:05:09,228 --> 00:05:11,063
The server brings the kernel.

157
00:05:11,504 --> 00:05:12,560
That's why an image is

158
00:05:12,642 --> 00:05:14,267
often tens of megabytes,

159
00:05:14,383 --> 00:05:15,533
while a virtual machine's

160
00:05:15,649 --> 00:05:18,017
disk is usually gigabytes.

161
00:05:18,783 --> 00:05:20,095
You describe an image with a

162
00:05:20,165 --> 00:05:22,370
short recipe called a Dockerfile.

163
00:05:22,754 --> 00:05:24,228
Each step adds a layer:

164
00:05:24,507 --> 00:05:25,784
a set of files stacked

165
00:05:25,819 --> 00:05:27,270
on top of the step before.

166
00:05:27,653 --> 00:05:28,698
Start from a base with

167
00:05:28,768 --> 00:05:30,265
Linux tools and Node.

168
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:32,541
Copy in your list of dependencies.

169
00:05:32,785 --> 00:05:33,644
Install them.

170
00:05:33,969 --> 00:05:35,594
Then copy in your code.

171
00:05:35,919 --> 00:05:38,334
Four steps, four layers.

172
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:40,888
Here's what most people don't know.

173
00:05:41,271 --> 00:05:43,291
Image layers are read-only.

174
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:44,742
They never change.

175
00:05:45,126 --> 00:05:46,484
When a container runs,

176
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:48,887
Docker adds one thin layer on top,

177
00:05:49,061 --> 00:05:50,315
just for that container.

178
00:05:50,756 --> 00:05:52,950
Any file the app changes is copied

179
00:05:52,985 --> 00:05:54,808
up into that top layer first.

180
00:05:55,133 --> 00:05:57,350
This trick is called copy-on-write.

181
00:05:57,734 --> 00:05:59,011
It's why ten containers

182
00:05:59,069 --> 00:06:00,334
can share one image,

183
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:01,727
and why a fresh container

184
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,223
always starts perfectly clean.

185
00:06:04,983 --> 00:06:07,177
Layers also make rebuilds fast.

186
00:06:07,502 --> 00:06:09,766
Docker keeps every layer it has built.

187
00:06:10,091 --> 00:06:11,775
Change one line of your code,

188
00:06:11,926 --> 00:06:13,621
and every step before copying

189
00:06:13,667 --> 00:06:15,978
your code is reused as-is.

190
00:06:16,361 --> 00:06:18,242
Only the last steps run again.

191
00:06:18,567 --> 00:06:20,506
That's why good Dockerfiles install

192
00:06:20,575 --> 00:06:22,816
dependencies before copying the code.

193
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:24,395
Do it the other way around,

194
00:06:24,453 --> 00:06:27,692
and every tiny edit reinstalls everything.

195
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:29,901
So let's put it all together.

196
00:06:30,226 --> 00:06:32,595
A container is a normal process.

197
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:35,091
Namespaces decide what it can see.

198
00:06:35,370 --> 00:06:37,645
Cgroups decide how much it can use.

199
00:06:37,889 --> 00:06:40,164
And an image gives it its own files.

200
00:06:40,443 --> 00:06:42,057
It isn't a tiny computer.

201
00:06:42,266 --> 00:06:43,740
It's an ordinary process

202
00:06:43,775 --> 00:06:45,632
that the kernel is lying to.

203
00:06:46,383 --> 00:06:48,275
Now watch docker run again.

204
00:06:48,601 --> 00:06:50,133
Docker downloads any image

205
00:06:50,191 --> 00:06:51,561
layers you don't have yet.

206
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,233
It stacks them and adds

207
00:06:53,268 --> 00:06:54,580
a thin writable layer.

208
00:06:54,858 --> 00:06:56,008
It puts on the blinders

209
00:06:56,054 --> 00:06:57,227
and sets the budget.

210
00:06:57,505 --> 00:06:58,736
Then it starts your app

211
00:06:58,806 --> 00:07:00,826
as one ordinary process.

212
00:07:01,104 --> 00:07:02,115
The same files on your

213
00:07:02,161 --> 00:07:03,566
laptop and on the server,

214
00:07:03,635 --> 00:07:05,493
so no more works on my machine.

215
00:07:05,702 --> 00:07:07,490
And no extra operating system,

216
00:07:07,548 --> 00:07:09,557
so it starts in about a second.

217
00:07:10,317 --> 00:07:11,130
Want proof?

218
00:07:11,513 --> 00:07:12,627
On a Linux server,

219
00:07:12,802 --> 00:07:14,752
run ps, the command that

220
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:16,621
lists every running program.

221
00:07:16,946 --> 00:07:19,187
Your container is right there in the list.

222
00:07:19,466 --> 00:07:20,905
Just a normal process,

223
00:07:20,998 --> 00:07:22,856
with a very convincing view.
